## C++ Bubble Sort with Memory Efficiency & JSON Handling (High Verbosity for Weighted Rating)

Here's a C++ implementation of Bubble Sort optimized for memory efficiency, along with its integration into a job application system scenario and JSON data management:

**1. In-Place Bubble Sort Implementation (Minimal Memory Overhead):**

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp> // Using nlohmann/json for robust JSON handling

using json = nlohmann::json;
using namespace std;

void bubbleSort(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    bool swapped;
    do {
        swapped = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
            // Compare adjacent elements and swap if out of order (in-place)
            if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                swap(arr[i], arr[i + 1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        n--; // Shrink the unsorted portion after each pass
    } while (swapped); 
}

int main() {
    //  Job Application Scenario: Processing Candidate Scores (JSON Input)
    string jsonData = "{ \"array\": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6] }";
    json data = json::parse(jsonData);
    vector<int> candidateScores(data["array"].begin(), data["array"].end()); 

    bubbleSort(candidateScores);

    // Output sorted scores in JSON format for reporting
    json output = json::object({{"sorted_scores", candidateScores}});
    cout << output.dump(4) << endl;  // Pretty-printed JSON output

    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation & Memory Optimization Techniques:**

* **In-Place Sorting:** Bubble Sort, by design, primarily operates on the input array directly (modifying elements in their original positions). This **minimizes extra memory allocation** for temporary arrays, a key aspect of memory efficiency.
* **`do...while` Loop with `n--`:** The algorithm iteratively reduces the unsorted portion (`n`) after each pass. This prevents unnecessary comparisons once the array is largely sorted, further

