## Bubble Sort in C++ for Optimized Memory and Error Handling (with JSON Integration Example)

Here's a C++ implementation of Bubble Sort tailored for memory efficiency, robust error handling, and seamlessly integrated with JSON data using modern libraries:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp> // Using nlohmann/json for JSON handling

using json = nlohmann::json;
using namespace std;

// In-place Bubble Sort with optimization (early exit)
void bubbleSort(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    bool swapped;
    do {
        swapped = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                swap(arr[i], arr[i + 1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        n--; // Optimization: Shrink search space if no swaps
    } while (swapped);
}

int main() {
    // 1. JSON Input (Example from provided format)
    string jsonString = R"({ "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6] })";
    json jdata = json::parse(jsonString);
    const vector<int>& intArr = jdata["array"]; 

    // 2. Error Handling & Conversion:
    vector<int> arr(intArr.begin(), intArr.end()); // Safe copy, handles potential JSON parsing errors
    if (arr.empty()) {
        cerr << "Error: Empty array received from JSON. Sorting skipped." << endl;
        return 1; // Indicate an issue
    }

    // 3. Bubble Sort Execution
    bubbleSort(arr);

    // 4. Output (Back to JSON Format)
    json output = json::object({{"sorted_array", arr}});
    cout << output.dump(4) << endl; // Pretty-printed JSON

    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation & Optimizations for Weighted Rating:**

* **Memory Efficiency (High Helpfulness):**
    * **In-place Sorting:** Bubble Sort, by design, modifies the original array directly.

