## Illustrating Natural Selection with MATLAB: Peppered Moth Analogy

While directly modeling *exact* survival probabilities within a tiny table like the initial one in pure MATLAB is limited, we can **simulate a compelling scenario mirroring natural selection** using the peppered moth classic example and demonstrate the concept clearly:

```matlab
% Environment Setup: Predation Pressure (Simulating "Favorable" Trait)
env_type = 'sooty'; %  Shifting to sooty environment favors darker moths
predation_rate_light = 0.6; % Higher predation on light moths in sooty areas
predation_rate_dark = 0.2; % Lower predation on dark moths

% Moth Population Initialization (Generational Representation)
generation = 1;
population(1).color = {'light', 'dark'}; % Initial mix
population(1).count = [200, 100]; % Numbers (adjust as needed for scale)

% Simulation Loop (Multiple Generations)
for gen = 1:10
    % Survival & Reproduction (Natural Selection in Action)
    survivors = zeros(1,2);
    for i = 1:length(population(gen).color)
        color = population(gen).color{i};
        survival_prob =  if strcmp(env_type, 'sooty')
                            predation_rate_dark  if strcmp(color, 'dark') else predation_rate_light end;
        survivors(i) = population(gen).count(i) * (1 - survival_prob); 
    end

    % Next Generation Counts (Inheritance)
    population(gen+1).count = survivors;
    population(gen+1).color = population(gen).color;  % Trait persists

    % Display (Optional - Visualize Change)
    fprintf('Generation %d: Light:%d, Dark:%d\n', gen, population(gen+1).count(1), population(gen+1).count(2));
end

% Analysis (Post-Simulation)
final_proportions = population(end).count ./ sum(population(end).count);
fprintf('\nFinal Proportion: Light: %.2f%%, Dark: %.2f%%\n', final_proportions(1)*100, final_proportions(2)*100);
```

